  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 326 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nNo major changes, please see previous discussion...\n\nInto the long term our temperatures will drop several degrees below \naverage under a colder airmass with some increasing chances for \nprecipitation, primarily for our eastern zones this time, with \ntemperatures then warming to near average by the middle of the work \nweek under brief clearing. The upper level pattern will see the \nnorthern trough quickly accelerating eastwards, leaving us under \nsouthwesterly flow aloft from a brief finger of weak transient \nridging that will quickly be erased by low pressure slowly \nprogressing across the Four Corners region. This will then become \nour main weather feature through Monday before finally sliding \neastwards into the Central Plains, with another transient ridge \nbuilding on Tuesday that will briefly hold the region before another \nPacific low moves into the Pacific Northwest and then starts to \nknock on our doorstep by Wednesday into Thursday. Under this \npattern, we should see surface flow begin to weaken by later \nSaturday and continue into the early part of next week as the \nstrongest part of the jet remains just south and west of the \nforecast area. With this flow, the best moisture will remain just \noutside of our forecast area to the east as well. That being said, \nPWAT values will still be on the higher side even for our area by \naround 0.15-0.25 inch, and scattered shower activity is expected \nfrom Saturday afternoon and continuing through Monday evening before \nexiting the region. The highest QPF amounts should align with the \nbest moisture for the southeastern portions of the Nebraska \nPanhandle, but some meager moisture should occur across most of the \nforecast area east of the Laramie Range. Morning lows both Sunday \nand Monday could also allow for this to mix with snow, but don't \nexpect noteworthy winter accumulations at this time. As conditions \nfinally clear later Monday into Tuesday and the transient ridge \nmoves in, 700mb temperatures will warm back into the 5-8 C range, \nallowing surface temperatures to rise back to near average in the 50-\n60 degree range. Tightening pressure gradients from the approaching \nPacific system will start to increase westerly to southwesterly \nwinds again for our western zones on Tuesday and Wednesday, but \notherwise expect conditions to be a bit more uneventful as \nprecipitation chances dwindle outside of the high terrain for the \nmiddle of the week.",
